sections.s2_technical:
The first and most important question is whether the capability is a core differentiator or commodity infrastructure. A core differentiator is something customers choose you for and competitors cannot easily copy, like a bank's proprietary fraud-detection model. Commodity infrastructure is a solved problem many vendors provide equally well, like sending email, storing blobs, or managing a message queue. You generally build differentiators and buy commodities.

The second factor is total cost of ownership, which goes far beyond initial development. Building includes ongoing maintenance, upgrades, security patches, scaling work, and the on-call burden of being paged when it breaks. Buying includes license or usage fees but offloads most of that operational weight to the vendor.

```java
// A rough total-cost-of-ownership comparison guides the decision
public String decide(boolean coreDifferentiator, double buildTco, double buyTco,
                     boolean teamHasExpertise, boolean urgentTimeToMarket) {
    if (coreDifferentiator && teamHasExpertise) return "BUILD (competitive edge)";
    if (urgentTimeToMarket && !coreDifferentiator) return "BUY (speed, commodity)";
    return buyTco <= buildTco ? "BUY (lower TCO)" : "BUILD (lower TCO)";
}
```

The third set of factors is risk: integration effort, vendor lock-in where switching later is costly, and data-sovereignty concerns where sending sensitive banking data to a third party may violate regulation. These can push a decision toward building or toward choosing a vendor that keeps data in-region. The fourth factor is team expertise and bandwidth: can the team not only build it but maintain it for years, and is that the best use of scarce engineering time. The fifth is time to market: buying is usually far faster to launch.

The reason "we can build it better" is often the most expensive decision is opportunity cost and the hidden long tail. Even if the team could build a marginally better message queue, every hour spent building and then maintaining it, patching it, scaling it, and being on-call for it is an hour not spent on the banking features that actually differentiate the business. A senior nuance is that the decision is not permanent: a sensible path is often to buy to get to market, then selectively build later only if a commodity becomes a genuine bottleneck or differentiator, and to prefer vendors with open standards to limit lock-in.

